  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You already know what a stack is elements go in, elements come out from the top, LIFO order. A monotonic stack adds&nbsp;<strong>one extra rule</strong>&nbsp;on top of that.",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-what-does-monotonic-mean-here",
      "title": "1. What Does Monotonic Mean Here?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A monotonic stack is a stack that is&nbsp;<strong>always sorted</strong>&nbsp;either always increasing from bottom to top, or always decreasing from bottom to top.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "1. Decreasing Stack",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In a&nbsp;<strong>decreasing stack</strong>, elements are arranged such that:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "From&nbsp;<strong>top → bottom</strong>, values keep&nbsp;<strong>increasing</strong><br>From&nbsp;<strong>bottom → top</strong>, values keep&nbsp;<strong>decreasing</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406116951-229353182.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Top →&nbsp;<code>1, 3, 5, 7</code>&nbsp;→ Bottom",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Here, the&nbsp;<strong>smallest element is at the top</strong>",
            "The&nbsp;<strong>largest element is at the bottom</strong>",
            "When pushing a new element, we&nbsp;<strong>pop all elements smaller than it</strong>&nbsp;to maintain decreasing order"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "<strong>Use case:<br></strong>Used when you want to find&nbsp;<strong>next greater element</strong>, or maintain dominance of larger elements.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "2. Increasing Stack",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In an&nbsp;<strong>increasing stack</strong>, elements are arranged such that:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "From&nbsp;<strong>top → bottom</strong>, values keep&nbsp;<strong>decreasing</strong><br>From&nbsp;<strong>bottom → top</strong>, values keep&nbsp;<strong>increasing</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196574-273128029.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196574-273128029.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196574-273128029.png",
          "alt": "Increasing Stack"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Example from image:<br>Top →&nbsp;<code>7, 5, 3, 1</code>&nbsp;→ Bottom",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Here, the&nbsp;<strong>largest element is at the top</strong>",
            "The&nbsp;<strong>smallest element is at the bottom</strong>",
            "When pushing a new element, we&nbsp;<strong>pop all elements greater than it</strong>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "<strong>Use case:<br></strong>Used when solving&nbsp;<strong>next smaller element</strong>, histogram problems, etc.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "3. Regular Stack",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A&nbsp;<strong>regular stack</strong>&nbsp;has&nbsp;<strong>no ordering constraint</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196576-5282590.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Example from image:<br>Top →&nbsp;<code>5, 1, 7, 3</code>&nbsp;→ Bottom",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Elements are stored&nbsp;<strong>as they come</strong>",
            "Only rule:&nbsp;<strong>LIFO (Last In, First Out)</strong>",
            "No sorting or monotonic property is maintained"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Use case:<br></strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">General stack operations like recursion, undo operations, expression evaluation.</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The sorted property is maintained&nbsp;<strong>at all times</strong>&nbsp;the moment a new element breaks the rule, you remove elements until the rule is restored.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-why-does-this-problem-even-need-a-special-technique",
      "title": "2. Why Does This Problem Even Need a Special Technique?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Consider this problem for each element in an array, find the&nbsp;<strong>next greater element</strong>&nbsp;to its right.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196576-99932476.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196576-99932476.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196576-99932476.png",
          "alt": "Why Does This Problem Even Need a Special Technique?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Array:&nbsp;<code>[4, 2, 6, 3, 5]</code><br>We process from&nbsp;<strong>right to left</strong>&nbsp;and use a stack.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "Step 1: Start with 5",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "First, we look at the last element, 5. Since there are no elements to its right, it does not have any greater element. So its answer is -1, and we put 5 into the stack.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "Step 2: Element = 3",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Next, we move to 3. The top of the stack is 5, which is greater than 3. So 5 is the next greater element for 3. We keep 5 in the stack and also push 3 onto the stack.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "Step 3: Element = 6",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Now we move to 6. The top of the stack is 3, which is smaller than 6, so we remove it because it is useless. Then we see 5, which is also smaller than 6, so we remove it as well. Now the stack is empty, meaning there is no greater element for 6. So its answer is -1, and we push 6 onto the stack.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "Step 4: Element = 2",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Next, we look at 2. The top of the stack is 6, which is greater than 2. So 6 is the next greater element for 2. We keep 6 and push 2 onto the stack.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "Step 5: Element = 4",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Finally, we look at 4. The top of the stack is 2, which is smaller than 4, so we remove it. Now the top is 6, which is greater than 4. So 6 is the next greater element for 4. Then we push 4 onto the stack.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "<span style=\"font-family: Charter, Georgia, Cambria, &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif; font-size: 21px; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: -0.003em; background-color: rgb(243, 244, 246);\">At the end, the answers become:</span><code style=\"font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">[6, 6, -1, 5, -1]</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "<div class=\"note-block\" data-type=\"note\" contenteditable=\"false\" style=\"margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid rgb(229, 231, 235);\"><div contenteditable=\"false\" style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; padding: 6px 16px; background: rgb(249, 250, 251); border-bottom-width: 1px; border-bottom-style: solid; border-bottom-color: rgb(229, 231, 235);\"><span style=\"font-size:11px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9ca3af\">Note</span></div><div contenteditable=\"true\" class=\"note-content\" style=\"padding: 10px 16px; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; color: rgb(55, 65, 81); outline: currentcolor;\"><h3>Key Idea</h3><p>At each step, remove smaller elements because they can never be the next greater for any element on the left. The remaining top of the stack gives the answer directly.</p></div></div><p>Brute force checks every element with all elements on its right, which repeats work and takes O(n²).</p><p>A monotonic stack avoids this by keeping only elements that are still waiting for a greater element.</p><p></p><hr style=\"border-width: 1px medium medium; border-style: solid none none; border-color: rgb(224, 224, 224) currentcolor currentcolor; border-image: none; margin: 24px 0px;\"><p></p><p></p>",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-the-core-idea",
      "title": "3. The Core Idea",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The idea is simple: when a new element comes and it is greater than the top of the stack, it becomes the answer for that top element. So we pop the top, store the answer, and repeat until the stack is valid.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each element is pushed once and popped once, so the total time is O(n).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196576-226078645.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196576-226078645.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196576-226078645.png",
          "alt": "The Core Idea"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Array:&nbsp;<code>[4, 2, 6, 3, 5]</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "We go from left to right and use a stack to track elements waiting for a greater value.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "First, 4 comes. The stack is empty, so we simply push 4. It is now waiting for a greater element.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Next, 2 comes. Since 2 is smaller than 4, it cannot help 4. So we push 2 as well. Now both 4 and 2 are waiting.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Then, 6 comes. Now 6 is greater than 2, so 6 becomes the next greater element for 2, and we remove 2.<br>6 is also greater than 4, so 6 becomes the next greater for 4, and we remove 4 as well.<br>Now the stack is empty, so we push 6.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Next, 3 comes. Since 3 is smaller than 6, it cannot resolve 6. So we push 3 and both are waiting.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Finally, 5 comes. It is greater than 3, so 5 becomes the next greater for 3, and we remove 3.<br>Now 5 is smaller than 6, so it cannot resolve 6. We push 5.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At the end, elements left in the stack (6 and 5) did not find any greater element, so their answers are -1.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Final answer:&nbsp;<code>[6, 6, -1, 5, -1]</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-two-types-of-monotonic-stack",
      "title": "4. Two Types of Monotonic Stack",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "1. Monotonic Increasing Stack",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Bottom to top values always&nbsp;<strong>increase</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196577-739156677.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196577-739156677.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196577-739156677.png",
          "alt": "Monotonic Increasing Stack"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When a new element arrives, remove all elements from the stack that are greater than the current element.<br>This is used for:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Finding the next smaller element",
            "Finding the previous smaller element"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "2. Monotonic Decreasing Stack",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Bottom to top values always&nbsp;<strong>decrease</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196577-666149280.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196577-666149280.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406196577-666149280.png",
          "alt": "Monotonic Decreasing Stack"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When a new element arrives, remove all elements from the stack that are smaller than the current element.<br>This is used for:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Finding the next greater element",
            "Finding the previous greater element"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
